::ALG1_U02_L06_Q01::A gym charges $15 plus $6 per class. The total is $57. Which equation and solution model the number of classes c? {
=A. 15 + 6c = 57, c = 7 #Correct. Subtract 15, then divide by 6.
~B. 15c + 6 = 57, c = 3.4 #This reverses the fixed fee and class cost.
~C. 6c = 57, c = 9.5 #This omits the fixed fee.
~D. 15 + 6c = 57, c = 42 #This stops before dividing by 6.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q02::A phone plan costs $25 plus $4 per extra gigabyte. The bill is $53. How many extra gigabytes g were used? {
~A. 4 #Subtracting the monthly cost was not completed.
=B. 7 #Correct. 25 + 4g = 53, so g = 7.
~C. 13.25 #This divides the total by 4 and ignores the monthly cost.
~D. 28 #This is the extra cost, not the number of gigabytes.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q03::Three less than twice a number is 17. Which equation is correct? {
~A. 3 - 2n = 17 #This means twice a number less than 3.
~B. 2(n - 3) = 17 #This subtracts 3 before doubling.
=C. 2n - 3 = 17 #Correct. Three less than twice a number is 2n - 3.
~D. 2n + 3 = 17 #This represents three more than twice a number.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q04::A rectangle has perimeter 42 inches and width 8 inches. Which equation can find the length l? {
~A. l + 8 = 42 #Perimeter uses two lengths and two widths.
~B. 8l = 42 #This models area, not perimeter.
~C. 2l + 8 = 42 #There are two widths, so use 16.
=D. 2l + 16 = 42 #Correct. Perimeter is 2l + 2w.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q05::A school club has $20 and earns $5 per ticket. The club has $75. How many tickets t were sold? {
=A. 11 #Correct. 20 + 5t = 75, so t = 11.
~B. 15 #This divides the total by 5 and ignores the starting amount.
~C. 55 #This is the earned amount before dividing by 5.
~D. 95 #This adds the amounts instead of solving.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q06::A taxi charges $4 to start and $3 per mile. The total is $25. Which equation and solution model the miles m? {
~A. 4m + 3 = 25, m = 5.5 #This reverses the fixed cost and per-mile cost.
=B. 4 + 3m = 25, m = 7 #Correct. Subtract 4, then divide by 3.
~C. 3 + 4m = 25, m = 5.5 #This reverses the fixed cost and per-mile cost.
~D. 4 + 3m = 25, m = 21 #This stops before dividing by 3.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q07::The sum of a number and 14 is 50. What is the number? {
~A. 64 #This adds 14 instead of subtracting it.
~B. 700 #This multiplies instead of using a sum.
=C. 36 #Correct. n + 14 = 50, so n = 36.
~D. 14 #This is the known addend, not the unknown.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q08::A student writes x = 9 for a word problem about dollars. Which interpretation is best? {
~A. The answer is nine equations. #The variable represents a quantity in the context, not equations.
~B. The answer is x. #The value must be interpreted in words.
~C. The answer has no units. #The context is about dollars.
=D. The unknown amount is 9 dollars. #Correct. Interpret the value using the context.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q09::A membership costs $12 plus $8 per month. The total cost is $60. How many months m are included? {
=A. 6 #Correct. 12 + 8m = 60, so m = 6.
~B. 7.5 #This divides the total by 8 and ignores the starting cost.
~C. 48 #This is the remaining cost, not the number of months.
~D. 72 #This adds 12 to the total.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q10::Four more than five times a number is 39. Which equation is correct? {
~A. 4n + 5 = 39 #This means five more than four times a number.
=B. 5n + 4 = 39 #Correct. Five times a number plus four is 39.
~C. 5(n + 4) = 39 #This adds before multiplying.
~D. 5n - 4 = 39 #This represents four less than five times a number.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q11::A savings account starts with $40 and increases by $12 each week. The balance is $136. How many weeks w have passed? {
~A. 11.3 #This divides the total by 12 and ignores the starting amount.
~B. 96 #This is the increase, not the number of weeks.
=C. 8 #Correct. 40 + 12w = 136, so w = 8.
~D. 176 #This adds the starting amount to the balance.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q12::A rectangle has perimeter 54 meters and width 10 meters. What is the length l? {
~A. 22 #This subtracts one width from half the perimeter instead of using both widths correctly.
~B. 44 #This subtracts a width but does not use the perimeter formula.
~C. 27 #This is half the perimeter, not the length.
=D. 17 #Correct. 2l + 20 = 54, so l = 17.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q13::A museum charges $24 as a group fee plus $6 per student. The total is $96. How many students s attended? {
=A. 12 #Correct. 24 + 6s = 96, so s = 12.
~B. 16 #This divides the total by 6 and ignores the group fee.
~C. 72 #This is the student-charge total, not the number of students.
~D. 120 #This adds the group fee to the total.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q14::A runner has completed 3 miles and runs 2 miles each day. The total is 17 miles. How many days d did the runner run? {
~A. 10 #This adds instead of subtracting the starting miles.
=B. 7 #Correct. 3 + 2d = 17, so d = 7.
~C. 8.5 #This divides the total by 2 and ignores the starting miles.
~D. 14 #This is the remaining miles, not the number of days.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q15::Four less than five times a number is 41. What is the number? {
~A. 7.4 #This divides after using the wrong operation.
~B. 8 #This gives 5n - 4 = 36, not 41.
=C. 9 #Correct. 5n - 4 = 41, so n = 9.
~D. 45 #This is the value before dividing by 5.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q16::A concert ticket order has a $9 service fee plus $18 per ticket. The total is $81. Which equation is correct? {
~A. 9t + 18 = 81 #This reverses the service fee and ticket cost.
~B. 18 + 9t = 81 #This reverses the service fee and ticket cost.
~C. 18t = 81 #This omits the service fee.
=D. 9 + 18t = 81 #Correct. Fixed fee plus ticket cost equals total.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q17::Five more than three times a number is 44. What is the number? {
=A. 13 #Correct. 3n + 5 = 44, so n = 13.
~B. 17 #This subtracts incorrectly after translating.
~C. 39 #This is the result after subtracting 5, not the number.
~D. 132 #This multiplies instead of solving.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q18::A student solves 7x + 3 = 31 and says the answer is 31. What is the mistake? {
~A. The student should have added 3 first. #The 3 should be subtracted.
=B. The student gave the total instead of solving for x. #Correct. The student used the total as the answer; subtract 3 and divide by 7 to get x = 4.
~C. The equation has no solution. #The equation has the solution x = 4.
~D. The student should have multiplied by 7 first. #The first inverse step is subtracting 3.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q19::A bus company charges $30 plus $2 per mile. A trip costs $110. How many miles m was the trip? {
~A. 55 #This divides the total by 2 and ignores the fixed charge.
~B. 80 #This is the variable cost, not the number of miles.
=C. 40 #Correct. 30 + 2m = 110, so m = 40.
~D. 140 #This adds the fixed charge to the total.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q20::Which statement best describes a constraint in a real-world equation problem? {
~A. It is the first number in every equation. #A constraint is not simply the first number.
~B. It is the answer choice with the largest value. #Constraints come from context, not size.
~C. It is a shortcut for solving without work. #Constraints do not replace solving.
=D. It is a condition that limits which answers make sense. #Correct. Context can restrict possible answers.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q21::A movie rental costs $3 plus $2 per day. The total is $13. How many days d was the movie rented? {
=A. 5 #Correct. 3 + 2d = 13, so d = 5.
~B. 6.5 #This divides the total by 2 and ignores the fixed cost.
~C. 10 #This is the remaining cost, not the number of days.
~D. 16 #This adds the fixed cost to the total.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q22::Which equation models "seven less than a number is 18"? {
~A. 7 - n = 18 #This means the number is subtracted from 7.
=B. n - 7 = 18 #Correct. Seven less than a number is n - 7.
~C. 7n = 18 #The phrase less than indicates subtraction, not multiplication.
~D. n + 7 = 18 #This means seven more than a number.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q23::A baker has 18 muffins and makes trays with 6 muffins each. The baker ends with 54 muffins. How many trays t were made? {
~A. 9 #This divides the final total by 6 and ignores the starting muffins.
~B. 36 #This is the added muffins, not the number of trays.
=C. 6 #Correct. 18 + 6t = 54, so t = 6.
~D. 72 #This adds the starting amount to the final amount.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q24::Which interpretation is correct if p = 4 in a problem where p represents packs of pencils? {
~A. The answer is 4 pencils per pack. #p represents packs, not pencils per pack.
~B. The answer is 4 equations. #The context is packs of pencils.
~C. The answer is p packs. #The variable should be replaced by its value.
=D. The answer is 4 packs of pencils. #Correct. Interpret the variable value in context.
}

::ALG1_U02_L06_Q25::Why is checking a solution important in a context problem? {
=A. It confirms the value satisfies the equation and makes sense in the situation. #Correct. Both algebra and context matter.
~B. It changes the equation into a formula. #Checking does not change the type of expression.
~C. It lets you skip defining the variable. #The variable should still be defined.
~D. It guarantees every answer choice is correct. #Checking identifies whether the solution works.
}
